<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dahomey Amazons</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Agojie, Mino</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazons</a> were the corps of women soldiers of the Kingdom of Dahomey, known locally as ahosi, &quot;king's wives&quot;, or mino, &quot;our mothers&quot;. Formed from palace guards in the eighteenth century and much expanded under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghezo">Ghezo</a>, they numbered several thousand at their height. They fought in Dahomey's wars with its neighbours and against the French in 1890 and 1892, and their reputation attracted wide European attention.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dahomey-amazons.jpg" alt="Dahomey Amazons" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dahomey_amazon2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
